WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:11.012
Your editor's undo button and a printer's job line look unrelated. One is a stack, the other is a queue, and interviews use both every day.

00:00:12.212 --> 00:00:13.140
Pause and think.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:16.594
Checking balanced brackets: stack or queue?

00:00:16.994 --> 00:00:24.308
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.539
Stacks and queues are two simple ways to line things up. Interviews use them constantly.

00:00:30.583 --> 00:00:39.650
New word: stack. Last-in-first-out structure; also the memory area holding function calls. Picture it like this: A stack of plates.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:46.355
New word: queue. First-in-first-out structure. Picture it like this: A line at a bus stop.

00:00:48.905 --> 00:00:55.012
A stack is a pile of plates. You add to the top and take from the top. Last in, first out.

00:00:55.462 --> 00:01:02.033
A queue is the line at a coffee shop. You join at the back and get served at the front. First in, first out.

00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:05.252
Watch a stack check brackets.

00:01:05.702 --> 00:01:08.860
We see an opening curly brace, so we push it.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.609
Then an opening square bracket, push.

00:01:12.059 --> 00:01:16.912
A closing square bracket must match the top plate. It does, so we pop.

00:01:17.362 --> 00:01:22.865
The closing curly brace matches the new top. Pop. The pile is empty, so the string is valid.

00:01:24.215 --> 00:01:28.719
A special trick is the monotonic stack: a stack kept in sorted order.

00:01:28.805 --> 00:01:41.251
New word: monotonic stack. A stack kept always increasing (or decreasing) to find next-greater elements fast. Picture it like this: A line of people where shorter people step out whenever a taller one arrives.

00:01:42.855 --> 00:01:47.221
For daily temperatures, the stack holds days still waiting for a warmer day.

00:01:47.671 --> 00:01:52.756
When a hotter day arrives, it pops every colder waiting day and gives them their answer.

00:01:53.206 --> 00:01:57.223
Each day is pushed once and popped once, so the whole thing is linear.

00:01:58.573 --> 00:01:59.502
Now a queue.

00:01:59.952 --> 00:02:01.542
Tasks join at the back.

00:02:01.992 --> 00:02:04.152
The front task is served and leaves.

00:02:04.602 --> 00:02:10.825
Breadth first search uses a queue so that nodes are processed in the order they were discovered, level by level.

00:02:10.988 --> 00:02:20.636
New word: node. One point in a graph or tree; also one machine in a cluster. Picture it like this: A city on a map, or one computer in a group.

00:02:23.138 --> 00:02:27.085
In driver code you use queues all the time, as ring buffers.

00:02:27.204 --> 00:02:35.633
New word: ring buffer. A fixed-size circular queue where the writer wraps around to the start. Picture it like this: A rotating sushi belt.

00:02:37.254 --> 00:02:42.490
A ring buffer is a fixed array where the head and tail indexes wrap around using modulo.

00:02:42.637 --> 00:02:51.077
New word: array. Items stored side by side in memory, reached by index. Picture it like this: A row of numbered lockers.

00:02:52.687 --> 00:02:56.611
Nothing is ever copied or shifted, so every operation is O of one.

00:02:57.061 --> 00:03:03.621
With one producer and one consumer, it can even be made lock free using atomic head and tail indexes.

00:03:03.737 --> 00:03:15.359
New word: atomic. An operation that happens all at once; no other thread can see it half-done. Picture it like this: Flipping a light switch — it's either on or off, never half.

00:03:17.887 --> 00:03:20.592
Common interview mistakes with stacks and queues.

00:03:21.042 --> 00:03:24.049
Popping from an empty stack. Always check first.

00:03:24.499 --> 00:03:28.667
Forgetting to check that the stack is empty at the very end of a bracket check.

00:03:29.117 --> 00:03:34.063
Using a Java Stack class instead of ArrayDeque, which is faster and the modern choice.

00:03:34.513 --> 00:03:41.595
And for ring buffers, mixing up full and empty when head equals tail. Keep a count, or leave one slot unused.

00:03:42.945 --> 00:03:46.962
Stacks for "most recent unfinished thing", queues for "oldest first".

00:03:46.932 --> 00:03:48.186
Back to the question.

00:03:48.586 --> 00:03:51.639
Checking balanced brackets: stack or queue?

00:03:52.039 --> 00:03:56.265
A stack, because the most recently opened bracket must close first.

00:03:57.465 --> 00:03:58.336
Quick recap.

00:03:58.736 --> 00:04:01.105
Stack: last in, first out.

00:04:01.505 --> 00:04:03.954
Queue: first in, first out.

00:04:04.354 --> 00:04:08.708
Brackets and undo use stacks; BFS and job lines use queues.
